===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# An [[English]] [[dessert]] made from a [[mixture]] of thick [[custard]], [[fruit]], [[sponge cake]], [[jelly]] and [[whipped cream]].
#: {{coordinate terms|en|tiramisu|bread pudding}}
# Anything that is of little importance or worth.
#: {{syn|en|bagatelle|minor detail|whiffle|Thesaurus:trifle}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello|III|3|text='''Trifles''' light as air<br>Are to the jealous confirmation strong<br>As proofs of holy writ.}}
#* {{RQ:Drayton Nimphidia|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A73861.0001.001|text=Olde ''{{w|Geoffrey Chaucer|Chaucer}}'' doth of ''Topas'' tell,<br>Mad ''{{w|François Rabelais|Rablais}}'' of ''Pantagruell'',<br>A latter third of ''Dowsabell'',<br>With such poore '''trifles''' playing:}}
#* {{RQ:Defoe Moll Flanders|page=34|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004843555.0001.000|text={{...}} when they had the Character and Honour of a Woman at their Mercy, often times made it their Jest, and at least look’d upon it as a '''Trifle''', and counted the Ruin of those, they had had their Will of, as a thing of no value.}}
#* {{RQ:Carroll Looking-Glass|4|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/12/12-h/12-h.htm|text=‘And all about a rattle!’ said Alice, still hoping to make them a little ashamed of fighting for such a '''trifle'''.}}
## An insignificant amount of money.
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-1|III|3|text=A '''trifle''', some eight-penny matter.}}
##* {{RQ:Austen Northanger Abbey|9|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/121/121-h/121-h.htm|text=He told her of horses which he had bought for a '''trifle''' and sold for incredible sums {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Conrad Lord Jim|30|page=311|url=https://archive.org/details/lordjimtale00conrrich/page/310|text=What’s eighty dollars? A '''trifle'''. An insignificant sum.}}
##* '''1975''', {{w|Saul Bellow}}, ''{{w|Humboldt's Gift|Humboldt’s Gift}}'', New York: Avon, 1976, p.{{nbsp}}462,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/humboldt00bell]</sup>
##*: “It was bad of me then not to send the fifteen hundred dollars. I assumed it would be a '''trifle'''.”<br>“Well, until a few months ago it was a '''trifle'''.”
# A very small amount (''of'' something).
#: {{syn|en|smidgen|Thesaurus:modicum}}
#* '''1742''', {{w|Daniel Defoe}}, ''{{w|A tour thro' the whole island of Great Britain|A tour thro’ the whole island of Great Britain}}'', London: J. Osborn ''et al.'', Volume 2, Letter II. Containing A Description of the City of London, p.{{nbsp}}90, footnote,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004843899.0001.002]</sup>
#*: This Line leaves out {{...}} ''Poplar'' and ''Black-wall'', which are indeed contiguous, a '''Trifle''' of Ground excepted, and very populous.
#* {{RQ:Alcott Little Women|1|2|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/514/514-h/514-h.htm|text=There was a good deal of rustling and whispering behind the curtain, a '''trifle''' of lamp smoke, and an occasional giggle from Amy {{...}}}}
#* '''1932''', {{w|Graham Greene}}, ''{{w|Stamboul Strain}}'', London: Heinemann, Part 4, p.{{nbsp}}180,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.458557]</sup>
#*: “Take just a '''trifle''' of French mustard {{...}}”
# A particular kind of [[pewter]].
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[utensil|Utensil]]s made from this particular kind of pewter.

